The current developer (not the original author) of crypton and tls
[states](https://kazu-yamamoto.hatenablog.jp/entry/2026/09/25/110313)
that "I am not a cryptography expert, so I cannot write code that is
both correct and performant." This begs the question: Does anyone
understand and has anyone reviewed the LLM generated code that they are
pouring into these libraries?

The point of cryptonite was to package up industry standard C
implementations of crypto functions in haskell. This is no longer
that, and as such, does not seem like a trustworthly package to use.

The point of the tls library is well, to be secure. It seems doubtful that
the haskell tls library is better than standard tls libraries like openssl
at this point in time.

I have lost confidence in the maintenance of these, and would like
git-annex to migrate away from depending on them. --[[Joey]] 

This is separate, though obviously not entirely disconnected from
[[/no_LLM_code]].

-----

Here is a plan:

* Switch git-annex to botan for hash functions. There is already a build
  flag, but it would be easier if botan bundled the C library in the
  haskell package. Which they have considered doing.  
  The `botan-default` branch makes the switch.
  (Waiting on a fix for https://github.com/joeyh/git-annex-wheel/
  test failure.)
* botan lacks skein and blake2, so would need to get those hashes another
  way
* Switch from http-client-tls to http-client-openssl.
* Switch from warp-tls to warp-s2n-tls? Alternatively, warp-tls is only used by
  the webapp and p2phttp; the webapp is mostly used on localhost and
  p2phttp is often used behind a server (eg forgejo) that can handle the
  https if it only supported http.
* aws depends on http-client-tls, but only uses it in its test suite;
  the library uses the Manager that the user passes in.
  So it would be possible to depend on aws w/o getting http-client-tls as a
  dependency, except it has a gratuitous dependency on it in the 
  Library stanza of the cabal file. Easily fixed.
* Avoid DAV depending on http-client-tls, by passing a Manager to
  mkDAVContext. (The context can be updated after the fact to use a
  http-client-tls manager, but it still uses http-client-tls first to make
  one.)
